-tier-ingest)"></span>ingest =E2=80=94 per agent, per date/quarter</span>
      <span class=3D"item"><span class=3D"swatch" style=3D"background:var(-=
-tier-derived)"></span>derived =E2=80=94 entity resolution, restatements</s=
pan>
      <span class=3D"item"><span class=3D"swatch" style=3D"background:var(-=
-tier-portfolio)"></span>portfolio =E2=80=94 the one live graph</span>
      <span class=3D"item"><span class=3D"swatch" style=3D"background:var(-=
-tier-risk)"></span>risk/veto =E2=80=94 orchestrator decisions</span>
    </div>

    <div class=3D"callout" style=3D"border-left-color: var(--tier-ingest); =
background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tier-ingest) 8%, var(--surface));">
      <span class=3D"lbl" style=3D"color: var(--tier-ingest)">Two modes of =
reaching data</span>
      <strong>You know which graph you want</strong> =E2=80=94 compute its =
URI from the template
      (<code>{agent}</code> + <code>{date}</code>, or <code>{quarter}</code=
>) and scope straight
      to it: <code>GRAPH &lt;urn:graph:ingest:SEMANTIC:2026-08-18&gt; { ...=
 }</code>. No lookup =E2=80=94
      the application already knows today's date and which agent it is.
      <strong>You don't</strong> =E2=80=94 e.g. "every score from the last =
90 days," spanning an unknown
      number of dated graphs. Leave the graph as a variable and filter on w=
hat's <em>inside</em>
      the triples instead: <code>GRAPH ?g { ?s :timestamp ?t } FILTER(?t &g=
t;=3D "2026-05-20"^^xsd:date)</code>.
      The graph name gives a coarse, free first cut; the <code>timestamp</c=
ode>/<code>validFrom</code>
      literal on the individual gives the precise one.
    </div>

    <p>
      Neither mode above narrows by <em>company</em> =E2=80=94 only by <em>=
when and who wrote it</em>.
      One `ingest:SEMANTIC:{date}` graph holds that day's sentiment score f=
or every asset in the
      universe, all five in the worked example, all ~500 at production scal=
e, sitting side by
      side. Narrowing to one company is a completely ordinary triple-patter=
n match layered on
      top =E2=80=94 the same match you'd write if named graphs didn't exist=
 at all. In
      <code>instances.trig</code> the link is written in the <em>same batch=
</em> as the score
      itself: <code>:AAPL :hasScoreObservation :Snap_AAPL_Sent_20260805</co=
de> sits right beside
      the snapshot it points to, inside that day's graph =E2=80=94 the Sema=
ntic Agent asserts the
      observation and the pointer to it together, since both are new as of =
that run. So the two
      axes compose in one query: <code>GRAPH</code> does the coarse cut, th=
e asset's own IRI does
      the fine one.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <pre class=3D"code-block"><code><span class=3D"kw">PREFIX</span> : &l=
t;https://thesis.local/kg/portfolio#&gt;

<span class=3D"kw">SELECT</span> ?rawValue ?normalizedScore ?timestamp
<span class=3D"kw">WHERE</span> {
  <span class=3D"kw">GRAPH</span> &lt;urn:graph:ingest:SEMANTIC:2026-08-18&=
gt; {   <span class=3D"cm"># coarse: which batch</span>
    :AAPL :hasScoreObservation ?snap .              <span class=3D"cm"># fi=
ne: which company</span>
    ?snap a :ScoreSnapshot ;
          :rawValue ?rawValue ;
          :normalizedScore ?normalizedScore ;
          :timestamp ?timestamp .
  }
}</code></pre>
      <figcaption><span class=3D"k">What this shows:</span> the graph claus=
e and the asset IRI are
      independent constraints inside the same query =E2=80=94 one picks the=
 day's semantic batch, the
      other picks the company within it. Drop the fixed graph and replace i=
t with a variable
      <code>?g</code> to instead get "AAPL's score, whichever day it was wr=
itten."</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p><strong>The three query patterns actually used by the agent layer:</=
strong></p>
    <ul class=3D"plain">
      <li><strong>"Active universe"</strong> =E2=80=94 <code>UniverseMember=
ship</code> / <code>PortfolioPosition</code>
      individuals with <strong>unbound</strong> <code>validTo</code>. This =
is exactly how
      <code>load_active_universe</code> starts every monitoring cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>"Latest snapshot per asset"</strong> =E2=80=94 for each (=
asset, metric type), take the
      individual with the newest <code>timestamp</code>. The docs describe =
this procedurally, not as
      a fixed query text =E2=80=94 it's the pattern to implement, not a sni=
ppet to copy verbatim.</li>
      <li><strong>"Everything this agent wrote on this day"</strong> =E2=80=
=94 scope a <code>SELECT</code> to a
      single <code>urn:graph:ingest:{agent}:{date}</code> graph. Writes go =
in the same way, via
      <code>INSERT DATA</code>/<code>INSERT</code> into that day's graph.</=
li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Two things that shape what a query can even see:</strong></p=
>
    <ul class=3D"plain">
      <li><strong>Reasoning is deliberately shallow.</strong> The store run=
s OWL 2&nbsp;RL/RDFS+, not
      full OWL&nbsp;DL. <code>rdfs:subClassOf</code> transitivity stays on,=
 so "every Asset in
      Information Technology" rolls up through Industry=E2=86=92Sector for =
free. Property-chain inference
      stays off =E2=80=94 <code>sharedExecutiveWith</code> (the contagion s=
ignal behind <code>VETO_RED_01</code>)
      is asserted explicitly in <em>both</em> directions by the entity-reso=
lution service rather
      than inferred, so every contagion edge in the graph is auditable back=
 to a writer, not a
      reasoner's guess.</li>
      <li><strong>SHACL never answers a query.</strong> <code>shapes.ttl</c=
ode> is a closed-world gate
      that <code>pyshacl</code> runs at ingest time =E2=80=94 it rejects ma=
lformed data before it lands.
      OWL (<code>tbox.ttl</code>) stays open-world and only ever adds infer=
ences; it never subtracts
      or validates. Don't expect a SHACL shape to filter a <code>SELECT</co=
de>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class=3D"callout">
      <span class=3D"lbl">Standing rule =E2=80=94 don't violate this one</s=
pan>
      Raw OHLCV never becomes triples. 500 tickers =C3=97 ~1,000 trading da=
ys =C3=97 5=E2=80=936 fields is
      low-semantic, high-volume tick data that would roughly double total t=
riple count for
      something almost never queried by IRI. It belongs in Parquet/SQLite. =
Only derived
      <code>PriceObservation</code> summaries =E2=80=94 close, return, ATR =
=E2=80=94 for a bounded window (e.g. a
      rolling 90 days) get projected into the graph.
    </div>
  </section>

